The index $
u_t = \inf\{k : T_k > t\}$ is a stopping time with finite mean. If $\mu < \infty$, Wald's equation gives $\mu\,\mathbb{E}
u_t = \mathbb{E} T_{
u_t} > t$, so $\liminf m(t)/t \ge 1/\mu$. The bound is trivial when $\mu = \infty$.

If $\xi_i \le c$ almost surely, then $T_{
u_t} = T_{
u_t - 1} + \xi_{
u_t} \le t + c$ because $T_{
u_t - 1} \le t$. Wald's equation then gives $\mu\,\mathbb{E}
u_t \le t + c$.
